## Changing a Username in RHEL 9 — Detailed Guide

### Prerequisites & Pre-Checks

Before making any changes, verify the current state of the account and ensure no sessions are active:

```bash
# Check current user details (UID, GID, groups, home, shell)
id old_username

# Check the /etc/passwd entry
getent passwd old_username

# Check for any running processes owned by the user
pgrep -u old_username -a

# Check for active login sessions
who | grep old_username
w old_username
```

If any sessions or processes exist, **terminate them all** before proceeding:

```bash
sudo pkill -u old_username
# If processes are stubborn:
sudo pkill -9 -u old_username
```

> **Critical:** You **cannot** rename the user you are currently logged in as. Always work from a **root** or **separate sudo-enabled** session.

---

### Step 1 — Rename the Login Name

```bash
sudo usermod -l new_username old_username
```

The `-l` (login) flag updates the username in `/etc/passwd` and `/etc/shadow`. **This alone does NOT rename the home directory** — the path in `/etc/passwd` still points to `/home/old_username`.

---

### Step 2 — Move the Home Directory

```bash
sudo usermod -d /home/new_username -m new_username
```

| Flag | Purpose |
|------|---------|
| `-d /home/new_username` | Sets the new home directory path in `/etc/passwd` |
| `-m` | **Moves** the contents of the old home directory to the new location (preserves permissions and ownership) |

> **Note:** `-m` only works if the old home directory still exists. If it was already deleted or the path doesn't match, you'll need to move it manually with `mv` first.

---

### Step 3 — Rename the Primary Group

On RHEL 9, `useradd` creates a **private primary group** with the same name as the user by default. If that's the case, rename it for consistency:

```bash
# Check if a group with the old name exists
getent group old_username

# If it exists, rename it
sudo groupmod -n new_username old_username
```

If the user's primary group is something else (e.g., `users`), skip this step.

---

### Step 4 — Update the GECOS / Comment Field (Optional)

The "real name" or comment field in `/etc/passwd` may still reference the old name:

```bash
sudo usermod -c "New Full Name" new_username
```

---

### Step 5 — Fix Remaining References

This is the part most people miss. Several system locations still reference the old username:

#### Crontab
```bash
# Check if a crontab exists for the old user
sudo crontab -u old_username -l 2>/dev/null

# If it does, you must copy it and delete the old one
sudo cp /var/spool/cron/old_username /var/spool/cron/new_username
sudo chown new_username:new_username /var/spool/cron/new_username
sudo rm /var/spool/cron/old_username
```

#### Mail Spool
```bash
# If the user has mail
sudo mv /var/spool/mail/old_username /var/spool/mail/new_username
sudo chown new_username:new_username /var/spool/mail/new_username
```

#### `at` Jobs
```bash
# Check for pending at jobs
atq
# Delete any jobs belonging to the old user by their job ID
```

#### Application Configs & Scripts
Search for any hardcoded references to the old username in configuration files, systemd units, or scripts:

```bash
sudo grep -r "old_username" /etc/ /opt/ /usr/local/ 2>/dev/null
```

#### Files Outside the Home Directory
If the user owned files outside `/home/old_username` (e.g., in `/var/log`, `/opt`, etc.), those files still have the **same UID** (which is good — ownership is preserved by numeric UID). But if you also changed the UID, you'd need:

```bash
sudo find / -uid <old_uid> -exec chown new_username {} \;
```

---

### Step 6 — Verify Everything

```bash
# Confirm the new entry in /etc/passwd
getent passwd new_username

# Confirm the group
getent group new_username

# Confirm UID/GID
id new_username

# Confirm home directory exists and is owned correctly
ls -ld /home/new_username

# Confirm the old name is gone
getent passwd old_username   # should return nothing
```

---

### Step 7 — Test the New Account

Log out and log back in as `new_username`. Verify:
- Home directory is accessible
- Shell works correctly
- Sudo works (if applicable): `sudo -l`
- Any critical applications or services function properly

---

### Common Pitfalls & Caveats

| Issue | Detail |
|-------|--------|
| **`usermod: user is currently used by process`** | Kill all processes for the user first, or reboot and run from single-user/recovery mode |
| **`usermod: no changes`** | The new name may already exist, or the old name was already updated |
| **IdM / FreeIPA managed users** | On RHEL 9 joined to an **Identity Management (IdM)** domain, local `usermod` changes will be **overwritten** on next sync. Use `idm user-mod` instead: `sudo idm user-mod --first=New --last=Name old_username` |
| **SELinux contexts** | Moving the home directory with `-m` should preserve SELinux labels, but verify with `ls -Z /home/new_username` if you use SELinux enforcing mode |
| **Backup first** | Always back up `/etc/passwd`, `/etc/shadow`, `/etc/group`, and `/etc/gshadow` before making changes: `sudo cp /etc/passwd /etc/passwd.bak` |
| **Username conventions** | `usermod` is less strict than `useradd` about naming rules, but stick to lowercase alphanumeric + hyphens/underscores, starting with a letter, max 32 chars |

---

### Quick Reference (One-Liner)

If you want to do it all in one shot (after ensuring the user is logged out):

```bash
sudo usermod -l new_username -d /home/new_username -m old_username && \
sudo groupmod -n new_username old_username && \
sudo usermod -c "New Full Name" new_username
```

Then handle crontabs, mail spool, and config files manually as described above.



